<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.3.4: Solving More Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00401</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.3.4: Solving More Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>53443ce7-7c39-4004-8ca7-56596f5210a7</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Solve this system of equations without graphing. Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>

  <para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}5x\;+\;y\;=\;7\\y\;=\;-20x\;+\;2\end{array}\right.\)</para>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the \(x\) value?</item>
</list>     
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(\frac15\)</para> 

           
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the \(y\) value?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>6</para>      



<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Explain your solutions.</item>
</list>     
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis></para>    
<para>Your answer may vary, but here are some samples.</para>
<list> 
  <item>Substituting \( 20x + 2 \) for \( y \) in the first equation, \( 5x+y=7 \), gives \( 5x + (20x+2) = 7 \) or \( 25x + 2 = 7 \), which then gives \( x=\frac15 \). Substituting \( \frac15 \) for \( x \) in the first equation and solving for \( y \) gives \( y=6 \).</item>
  <item>The first equation, \( 5x+y=7 \), can be rearranged to \( y = 7 - 5x \). Substituting \( 7-5x \) for \( y \) in the second equation gives \( 20x + 2 = 7-5x \). Solving this equation leads to \( x=\frac15 \), and substituting \( \frac15 \) for \( x \) in either equation gives \( y=6 \).</item>
</list> 
<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>Points to Emphasize</title>
<para>In a subsequent lesson, highlight both ways to substitute and invite students to discuss the benefits or potential challenges in each. (The lesson synthesis for Lesson 4 is a place where substitution may be discussed again.)</para>
</section>
</content>
